"[2604.04696] GPIR: Enabling Practical Private Information Retrieval with GPUs (https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.04696)\nciteturn2view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.04696\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 171\nL0: 【0†Search】 【1†Submit】 【2†Donate†info.arxiv.org】 【3†Log in】\nL1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · 【4†Advanced search】\nL5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Cryptography and Security\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 6 Apr 2026 (【5†v1】), last revised 3 May 2026 (this version, v2)]\nL9: # Title:GPIR: Enabling Practical Private Information Retrieval with GPUs\nL10: \nL11: Authors:【6†Hyesung Ji】, 【7†Hyunah Yu】, 【8†Jongmin Kim】, 【9†Wonseok Choi】, 【10†G. Edward Suh】, 【11†Jung Ho Ahn】\nL12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled GPIR: Enabling Practical Private Information Retrieval with GPUs, by Hyesung Ji and Hyunah Yu and Jongmin Kim and Wonseok Choi and G. Edward Suh and Jung Ho Ahn\nL14: \nL15: 【12†View PDF】 【13†HTML (experimental)】\nL16: > Abstract:Private information retrieval (PIR) allows private database queries; however, it is hindered by intense server-side computation and memory traffic. Numerous modern lattice-based PIR protocols consist of three phases: ExpandQuery (expanding a query into encrypted indices), RowSel (encrypted row selection), and ColTor (recursive \"column tournament\" for final selection).\nL17: ExpandQuery and ColTor primarily perform number-theoretic transforms (NTTs), whereas RowSel reduces to large-scale independent matrix-matrix multiplications (GEMMs). GPUs are well suited for these tasks when combined with multi-client batching, which is necessary for high throughput.\nL18: However, batching fundamentally reshapes the performance bottlenecks: while it amortizes database access costs, it expands working sets beyond the L2 cache capacity, causing divergent memory access behavior and excessive DRAM traffic. We present GPIR, a GPU-accelerated PIR system that rethinks kernel design, data layout, and execution scheduling.\nL19: We introduce a stage-aware hybrid execution model that dynamically switches between operation-level kernels, which execute each primitive operation separately, and stage-level kernels, which fuse all operations within a stage into a single kernel to maximize on-chip data reuse. For RowSel, we resolve the mismatch between NTT-driven layouts and tiled GEMMs using a transposed-layout design with fine-grained pipelining.\nL20: We further extend GPIR to multi-GPU systems, scaling throughput and database capacity with negligible communication overhead. GPIR achieves up to 297.2x higher throughput than PIRonGPU, the state-of-the-art GPU implementation.\nL21: Comments:  | 13 pages, 13 figures, accepted at ICS 2026\nL22: Subjects:  | Cryptography and Security (cs.CR); Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)\nL23: Cite as:  | 【14†arXiv:2604.04696】 [cs.CR]\nL24:    | (or 【15†arXiv:2604.04696v2】 [cs.CR] for this version)\nL25:    | 【16†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2604.04696†doi.org】 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL26: Related DOI (Digital Object Identifier):  | 【17†https://doi.org/10.1145/3797905.3807844†doi.org】 DOI(s) linking to related resources\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2606.16146] When Proofs Meet Hardware: Comparing NTT and SumCheck in Zero-Knowledge Systems (https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.16146)\nciteturn2view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.16146\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 167\nL0: 【0†Search】 【1†Submit】 【2†Donate†info.arxiv.org】 【3†Log in】\nL1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · 【4†Advanced search】\nL5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Hardware Architecture\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 15 Jun 2026]\nL9: # Title:When Proofs Meet Hardware: Comparing NTT and SumCheck in Zero-Knowledge Systems\nL10: \nL11: Authors:【5†Jianqiao Mo】, 【6†Alhad Daftardar】, 【7†Barath GaneshKumar】, 【8†Kaiyue Guo】, 【9†Hong Wang】, 【10†Benedikt Bunz】, 【11†Siddharth Garg】, 【12†Brandon Reagen】\nL12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled When Proofs Meet Hardware: Comparing NTT and SumCheck in Zero-Knowledge Systems, by Jianqiao Mo and 7 other authors\nL14: \nL15: 【13†View PDF】 【14†HTML (experimental)】\nL16: > Abstract:In the ZKP community, it has long been discussed that the SumCheck protocol is asymptotically more efficient than the Number Theoretic Transform (NTT), requiring only $O(N)$ arithmetic versus $O(N \\log N)$. At the same time, hardware accelerator designers propose that NTT is more hardware-friendly, benefiting from locality and data reuse, while SumCheck suffers from sequential, dependent rounds.\nL17: Despite these competing intuitions, the hardware-system-level trade-offs between NTT- and SumCheck-based proving primitives remain insufficiently understood.\nL18: > Beyond individual accelerator design, this work presents, to our knowledge, the first hardware-system-level direct comparison of NTT- and SumCheck-based proving primitives under a unified architectural framework. We study them in the context of the ZeroCheck protocol, a common building block in zkSNARKs. We implement optimized systems for both primitives. Both are evaluated under the same level on-chip SRAM and off-chip bandwidth budgets. Our results show that there is no universal winner.\nL19: Generally, SumCheck outperforms NTT for high-degree polynomials. For low-degree polynomials, performance depends on memory availability: under given SRAM budgets, NTT might deliver better performance for medium-sized workloads by exploiting data reuse.\nL20: > These findings, bridging cryptographic protocol design and hardware architecture, offer practical guidance for understanding the proving cost of NTT- and SumCheck-based zero-knowledge proof systems.\nL21: Subjects:  | Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)\nL22: Cite as:  | 【15†arXiv:2606.16146】 [cs.AR]\nL23:    | (or 【16†arXiv:2606.16146v1】 [cs.AR] for this version)\nL24:    | 【17†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2606.16146†doi.org】 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Jianqiao Cambridge Mo [【18†view email】]\nL28: [v1] Mon, 15 Jun 2026 03:03:54 UTC (430 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled When Proofs Meet Hardware: Comparing NTT and SumCheck in Zero-Knowledge Systems, by Jianqiao Mo and 7 other authors\nL35: \nL36:   * 【13†View PDF】\nL37:   * 【14†HTML (experimental)】\nL38:   * 【19†TeX Source】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[1601.03184] Multiple right-hand-side setup for the DD-αAMG (https://arxiv.org/abs/1601.03184)\nciteturn2view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1601.03184\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 162\nL0: 【0†Search】 【1†Submit】 【2†Donate†info.arxiv.org】 【3†Log in】\nL1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · 【4†Advanced search】\nL5: \nL6: # High Energy Physics - Lattice\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 13 Jan 2016]\nL9: # Title:Multiple right-hand-side setup for the DD-αAMG\nL10: \nL11: Authors:【5†Daniel Richtmann】, 【6†Simon Heybrock】, 【7†Tilo Wettig】\nL12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Multiple right-hand-side setup for the DD-\\alpha AMG, by Daniel Richtmann and 2 other authors\nL14: \nL15: 【8†View PDF】 【9†HTML (experimental)】\nL16: > Abstract:The setup cost of a modern solver such as DD-\\alpha AMG (Wuppertal Multigrid) is a significant contribution to the total time spent on solving the Dirac equation, and in HMC it can even be dominant. We present an improved implementation of this algorithm with modified computation order in the setup procedure. By processing multiple right-hand sides simultaneously we can alleviate many of the performance issues of the default single right-hand-side setup.\nL17: The main improvements are as follows: By combining multiple right-hand sides the message size for off-chip communication is larger, which leads to better utilization of the network bandwidth. Many matrix-vector products are replaced by matrix-matrix products, leading to better cache reuse. The synchronization overhead inflicted by on-chip parallelization (threading), which is becoming crucial on many-core architectures such as the Intel Xeon Phi, is effectively reduced.\nL18: In the parts implemented so far, we observe a speedup of roughly 3x compared to the optimized version of the single right-hand-side setup on realistic lattices.\nL19: Comments:  | 7 pages, 2 figures, Proceedings of Lattice 2015\nL20: Subjects:  | High Energy Physics - Lattice (hep-lat); Computational Physics (physics.comp-ph)\nL21: Cite as:  | 【10†arXiv:1601.03184】 [hep-lat]\nL22:    | (or 【11†arXiv:1601.03184v1】 [hep-lat] for this version)\nL23:    | 【12†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.1601.03184†doi.org】 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Daniel Richtmann [【13†view email】]\nL27: [v1] Wed, 13 Jan 2016 09:47:07 UTC (47 KB)\nL28: \nL29: Full-text links:\nL30: \nL31: ## Access Paper:\nL32: \nL33: View a PDF of the paper titled Multiple right-hand-side setup for the DD-\\alpha AMG, by Daniel Richtmann and 2 other authors\nL34: \nL35:   * 【8†View PDF】\nL36:   * 【9†HTML (experimental)】\nL37:   * 【14†TeX Source】\nL38: \nL39: 【15†view license】\nL40: ### Current browse context:\nL41: \nL42: hep-lat\nL43: \nL44: 【16†< prev】   |   【17†next >】\nL45: \nL46: 【18†new】 | 【19†recent】 | 【20†2016-01】\nL47: \nL48: Change to browse by:\nL49: \nL50: 【21†physics】\nL51: 【22†physics.comp-ph】\nL52: \nL53: ### References & Citations\nL54: \nL55:   * 【23†INSPIRE HEP†inspirehep.n